.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions manipulate ELF symbol table entries within an
.Vt elfobj_t
handle.
Symbols represent named entities in an ELF file: functions, variables,
sections, and other objects.
.Ss Symbol Binding
The
.Fa bind
argument to
.Fn elf_add_symbol
specifies the symbol's binding, which determines linkage visibility:
.Bl -tag -width "STB_WEAK"
.It Dv STB_LOCAL
Symbol is local to the object file.
Local symbols are not visible to the linker and cannot be referenced
from other object files.
All local symbols must appear before global symbols in the symbol table.
.It Dv STB_GLOBAL
Symbol is globally visible.
If multiple object files define a global symbol with the same name,
the linker will report a multiple-definition error unless one is
a common symbol or weak symbol.
.It Dv STB_WEAK
Symbol is weakly bound.
Weak symbols are similar to global symbols but have lower precedence.
If both a global and weak symbol with the same name exist, the global
symbol takes precedence.
Weak symbols are used for optional functionality and default implementations.
.El
.Ss Symbol Types
The
.Fa type
argument to
.Fn elf_add_symbol
specifies the symbol's type:
.Bl -tag -width "STT_SECTION"
.It Dv STT_NOTYPE
Symbol type is unspecified or unknown.
.It Dv STT_OBJECT
Symbol is a data object (variable, array, etc.).
.It Dv STT_FUNC
Symbol is a function or executable code.
.It Dv STT_SECTION
Symbol is associated with a section.
Section symbols are used primarily for relocations and have no name.
.It Dv STT_FILE
Symbol gives the name of the source file.
File symbols are local and appear at the beginning of the symbol table.
.It Dv STT_TLS
Symbol is thread-local storage.
.El
.Ss Symbol Visibility
Symbol visibility determines how the symbol is exported from a shared
library or executable.
Visibility is stored in the symbol's
.Va other
field (st_other) and can have the following values:
.Bl -tag -width "STV_PROTECTED"
.It Dv STV_DEFAULT
Default visibility.
Symbol is exported and can be interposed by another definition.
.It Dv STV_INTERNAL
Symbol is not visible outside the defining module.
.It Dv STV_HIDDEN
Symbol is not visible to other modules but may be accessed via
a pointer.
.It Dv STV_PROTECTED
Symbol is exported but cannot be interposed.
.El
Use
.Fn elf_symbol_set_visibility
to set visibility.
.Ss Functions
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fn elf_add_symbol "obj" "name" "value" "size" "bind" "type"
Creates a new symbol with the given attributes.
The symbol is appended to the object's symbol table.
Returns a pointer to the new symbol, or NULL on failure.
.Pp
The object must be mutable (created with
.Fn elf_create
and not yet finalized).
.Pp
Adding a global symbol with a name that already exists as a global
or weak symbol will fail with
.Dv ELF_ERR_FORMAT .
.It Fn elf_find_symbol "obj" "name"
Searches for a symbol by name.
Returns the symbol handle if found, or NULL if no symbol with the
given name exists.
.Pp
If multiple symbols have the same name (which can happen with local
symbols), the first match is returned.
.It Fn elf_symbol_at "obj" "index"
Returns the symbol at a specific index, or NULL if out of range.
.It Fn elf_symbol_define "symbol" "section" "value"
Marks a symbol as defined in a section at the given value.
.It Fn elf_symbol_set_binding "symbol" "bind"
Updates a symbol binding (local/global/weak).
.It Fn elf_symbol_set_type "symbol" "type"
Updates a symbol type.
.It Fn elf_symbol_set_visibility "symbol" "visibility"
Updates symbol visibility (st_other).
.It Fn elf_symbol_set_version "symbol" "version_index"
Assigns version metadata index for versioned symbols.
.It Fn elf_symbol_version "symbol"
Returns the version metadata index.
.It Fn elf_symbol_set_shndx "symbol" "shndx"
Sets section index, including
.Dv SHN_UNDEF ,
.Dv SHN_ABS ,
and
.Dv SHN_COMMON .
.It Fn elf_symbol_is_duplicate_global "obj" "name" "bind"
Checks if adding the given symbol would create a global/weak conflict.
.It Fn elf_symbols_sort_deterministic "obj" "first_global_out"
Applies deterministic symbol ordering with all local symbols first.
.It Fn elf_symbol_lookup_sysv "obj" "name"
Looks up symbol by name using SYSV hash-assisted matching.
.It Fn elf_symbol_lookup_gnu "obj" "name"
Looks up symbol by name using GNU hash-assisted matching.
.It Fn elf_symbol_count "obj"
Returns the number of symbols in the object's symbol table.
.It Fn elf_symbol_name "symbol"
Returns the symbol name.
The returned string is owned by the object and must not be freed.
May return an empty string for unnamed symbols (e.g., section symbols).
.It Fn elf_symbol_bind "symbol"
Returns the symbol binding value (for example
.Dv STB_LOCAL ,
.Dv STB_GLOBAL ,
or
.Dv STB_WEAK ) .
.It Fn elf_symbol_type "symbol"
Returns the symbol type value (for example
.Dv STT_NOTYPE ,
.Dv STT_OBJECT ,
.Dv STT_FUNC ,
.Dv STT_SECTION ,
or
.Dv STT_FILE ) .
.It Fn elf_symbol_shndx "symbol"
Returns the symbol section index (for example
.Dv SHN_UNDEF ,
.Dv SHN_ABS ,
or
.Dv SHN_COMMON ) .
.It Fn elf_symbol_value "symbol"
Returns the symbol value.
For defined symbols, this is typically the virtual address or offset.
For undefined symbols, this is typically 0.
.It Fn elf_symbol_size "symbol"
Returns the symbol size in bytes.
A size of 0 indicates that the size is unknown or unspecified.
.It Fn elf_hash_sysv "name"
Computes the SYSV hash of a symbol name.
This hash function is used by the
.Dv DT_HASH
dynamic symbol table hash table.
.It Fn elf_hash_gnu "name"
Computes the GNU hash of a symbol name.
This hash function is used by the
.Dv DT_GNU_HASH
dynamic symbol table hash table, which provides better performance
than SYSV hash for large symbol tables.
.El
.Ss Symbol Resolution Semantics
When linking multiple object files, symbols are resolved according
to the following rules:
.Bl -enum
.It
Local symbols are always resolved within the same object file.
.It
Global symbols take precedence over weak symbols with the same name.
.It
If multiple global symbols with the same name exist, the linker
reports a multiple-definition error.
.It
Common symbols
.Pq symbols with type STT_OBJECT and section index SHN_COMMON
are merged according to their alignment and size.
.It
Archive members are extracted only if they satisfy an undefined
global or weak symbol reference.
.El
.Ss Versioned Symbols
Symbol versioning allows multiple versions of a symbol to coexist
in a shared library.
Versioned symbols have an associated version index stored in the
.Va ver_index
field.
.Pp
The current version of
.Nm libelfobj
has limited support for versioned symbols:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Version indices can be read from input files.
.It
The
.Dv .gnu.version
and
.Dv .gnu.version_r
sections are not automatically processed.
.It
Creating versioned symbols requires manual version index assignment.
.El
.Ss Hash Tables
Symbol hash tables enable fast symbol lookup in dynamic linking.
Two hash table formats are supported:
.Bl -tag -width "GNU hash"
.It SYSV hash
The traditional ELF hash table, stored in a
.Dv DT_HASH
entry.
Uses the
.Fn elf_hash_sysv
function.
Provides O(1) average-case lookup.
.It GNU hash
An optimized hash table format, stored in a
.Dv DT_GNU_HASH
entry.
Uses the
.Fn elf_hash_gnu
function.
Provides better cache locality and faster lookup for large symbol tables.
.El
.Pp
When creating shared libraries, the library automatically generates
the appropriate hash table based on the symbol set.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn elf_add_symbol
and
.Fn elf_find_symbol
return a pointer to a symbol handle on success, or NULL on failure.
.Pp
.Fn elf_symbol_count
returns the number of symbols (may be 0 for an empty object).
.Pp
.Fn elf_symbol_name ,
.Fn elf_symbol_bind ,
.Fn elf_symbol_type ,
.Fn elf_symbol_shndx ,
.Fn elf_symbol_value ,
and
.Fn elf_symbol_size
return the requested attribute.
For NULL symbol pointers,
.Fn elf_symbol_name
returns NULL,
.Fn elf_symbol_bind
returns
.Dv STB_LOCAL ,
.Fn elf_symbol_type
returns
.Dv STT_NOTYPE ,
.Fn elf_symbol_shndx
returns
.Dv SHN_UNDEF ,
and numeric accessors return 0.
.Pp
.Fn elf_hash_sysv
and
.Fn elf_hash_gnu
return a 32-bit hash value.
For NULL input, 0 is returned.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn elf_add_symbol
may fail with:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Dv ELF_ERR_OOM
Memory allocation failed.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_STATE
The object is read-only or already finalized.
.It Dv ELF_ERR_FORMAT
A global symbol with the same name already exists.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Adding Symbols to an Object
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>

int main(void) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_section_t *text;
    elf_symbol_t *main_sym, *helper_sym;
    unsigned char code[] = { 0xC3 };  /* ret */

    obj = elf_create(ET_REL, EM_386, ELFOBJ_CLASS_32, ELFOBJ_ENDIAN_LE);
    if (!obj) return 1;

    text = elf_add_section(obj, ".text", SHT_PROGBITS,
                           SHF_ALLOC | SHF_EXECINSTR);
    if (!text) goto err;
    if (elf_section_set_data(text, code, sizeof(code)) != ELF_OK)
        goto err;

    /* Add a global function symbol */
    main_sym = elf_add_symbol(obj, "main", 0, sizeof(code),
                              STB_GLOBAL, STT_FUNC);
    if (!main_sym) goto err;

    /* Add a local helper function symbol */
    helper_sym = elf_add_symbol(obj, "helper", 0, 0,
                                STB_LOCAL, STT_FUNC);
    if (!helper_sym) goto err;

    if (elf_write_file(obj, "symbols.o") != ELF_OK) goto err;
    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;

err:
    elf_close(obj);
    return 1;
}
.Ed
.Ss Resolving Symbols Across Objects
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    elfobj_t *obj1, *obj2;
    elf_symbol_t *sym;
    elf_err_t err;

    if (argc < 3) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <obj1> <obj2>\\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    err = elf_open(argv[1], &obj1);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Cannot open %s: %s\\n", argv[1], elf_errstr(err));
        return 1;
    }

    err = elf_open(argv[2], &obj2);
    if (err != ELF_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Cannot open %s: %s\\n", argv[2], elf_errstr(err));
        elf_close(obj1);
        return 1;
    }

    /* Look for a symbol in the first object */
    sym = elf_find_symbol(obj1, "main");
    if (sym) {
        printf("Found 'main' in %s:\\n", argv[1]);
        printf("  Value: 0x%llx\\n",
               (unsigned long long)elf_symbol_value(sym));
        printf("  Size: %llu\\n",
               (unsigned long long)elf_symbol_size(sym));
    }

    /* Check if the same symbol exists in the second object */
    sym = elf_find_symbol(obj2, "main");
    if (sym) {
        printf("Found 'main' in %s:\\n", argv[2]);
        printf("  Value: 0x%llx\\n",
               (unsigned long long)elf_symbol_value(sym));
        printf("  Size: %llu\\n",
               (unsigned long long)elf_symbol_size(sym));
    }

    elf_close(obj1);
    elf_close(obj2);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Ss Using Hash Functions
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    const char *names[] = { "main", "printf", "malloc", NULL };

    for (int i = 0; names[i]; i++) {
        uint32_t sysv = elf_hash_sysv(names[i]);
        uint32_t gnu = elf_hash_gnu(names[i]);

        printf("%-10s: SYSV=0x%08x GNU=0x%08x\\n",
               names[i], sysv, gnu);
    }

    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Ss Creating Weak Symbols for Optional Dependencies
.Bd -literal
#include <elfobj.h>

int main(void) {
    elfobj_t *obj;
    elf_symbol_t *weak_sym;

    obj = elf_create(ET_REL, EM_386, ELFOBJ_CLASS_32, ELFOBJ_ENDIAN_LE);
    if (!obj) return 1;

    /* Create a weak symbol for an optional function.
     * The linker will use this definition only if no strong
     * definition is found. */
    weak_sym = elf_add_symbol(obj, "optional_hook", 0, 0,
                              STB_WEAK, STT_FUNC);
    if (!weak_sym) {
        elf_close(obj);
        return 1;
    }

    /* The object can now be linked with another object that
     * provides a strong definition of optional_hook, or used
     * as-is with the weak definition. */

    elf_close(obj);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
